5. Wizard Architecture
Each operational capability is implemented as a Wizard.

A wizard is a controlled workflow step that can:

• collect inputs
• validate rules
• call AI agents
• emit system events

Wizards do not directly invoke other wizards.

Instead they emit events which orchestrators consume.

6. Event-Driven Workflow Architecture
ALIS uses event-driven orchestration.

Example:

Student submits application
↓
Wizard emits APPLICATION_SUBMITTED
↓
Admissions Orchestrator receives event
↓
AI agents analyze application
↓
Eligibility rule engine evaluates result
↓
APPLICATION_EVALUATED event emitted
This ensures:

• modular workflows
• traceable transitions
• cross-module automation

7. Orchestrator Architecture
ALIS uses module-based orchestration.

Each module contains its own orchestrator.

Examples:

• Admissions Orchestrator
• Academics Orchestrator
• Exams Orchestrator

There is also a lightweight Global Event Supervisor that handles institution-wide events.

Example global events:

• semester start
• semester end
• policy updates
• financial deadlines

8. AI Architecture
AI operates through a central AI Gateway.

Flow:

Wizard / Orchestrator
↓
AI Gateway
↓
Agent Runtime
↓
Tool Invocation
↓
Local LLM Runtime
AI never interacts with the database directly.

9. AI Agents
Agents are defined actors with specific capabilities.

Examples:

• CourseBuilderAgent
• QuestionPaperAgent
• FeedbackSynthesizerAgent

Each agent has:

• allowed tools
• allowed modules
• model tier
• execution mode

Agents cannot write to the database.

10. Prompt Registry
All prompts are stored in a versioned Prompt Registry.

No prompt may be hardcoded in application code.

Prompt lifecycle:

DRAFT → REVIEW → ACTIVE → DEPRECATED
Prompts are rendered using Jinja2 templates.

11. AI Invocation Contract
Every AI execution must include:

• agent_id
• prompt_version
• model_version
• toolset_version
• tenant_id
• invoked_by
• policy_version
• input_hash
• timestamp

This guarantees replayable AI decisions.

12. Tool Invocation Framework
Agents interact with the system through deterministic tools.

Example tools:

• retrieve applicant data
• fetch academic policy
• validate grading rubric

Tools must:

• be read-only
• deterministic
• stateless

13. RAG Architecture
ALIS uses Retrieval Augmented Generation for institutional knowledge.

Sources include:

• academic policies
• regulatory guidelines
• course material
• research papers

Pipeline:

Query
↓
Embedding
↓
Vector database
↓
Context retrieval
↓
LLM reasoning
14. Security Model
ALIS enforces strict governance through:

• RBAC
• policy registry
• authority middleware
• immutable audit logs

AI outputs must always pass through the rule engine.

15. Audit Architecture
All operations produce immutable logs.

Example logged fields:

• actor_id
• event_type
• entity_id
• timestamp
• policy_version
• model_version
• prompt_version

Audit logs are stored in PostgreSQL.

16. Infrastructure Architecture
ALIS is designed for on-prem deployment.

Typical deployment:

Application Server
PostgreSQL Database
Vector Database
AI Gateway
Local LLM Runtime
GPU Inference Server
17. Hardware Model
Recommended baseline for mid-sized university:

• 4U rack server
• 2 GPUs (48GB VRAM recommended)
• 128GB system RAM
• NVMe storage

LLM runtime examples:

• vLLM
• Ollama
• llama.cpp

18. AI Model Strategy
ALIS uses a tiered model pool.

Example:

Tier 1 – Lightweight reasoning
(Qwen 1.5B)

Tier 2 – Deep reasoning
(LLaMA 7B)

Modules select model tiers based on task complexity.

19. Policy Management
Institutional rules are managed via a Policy Registry.

Example policies:

• attendance threshold
• grading rules
• scholarship caps
• eligibility criteria

Policies are versioned and enforced by the rule engine.

20. Configuration Boundary
Universities may configure:

• policies
• workflows
• grading formulas
• financial rules

They cannot modify:

• core system logic
• audit architecture
• state machines

21. AI Safety Model
AI is constrained through:

• read-only architecture
• tool access restrictions
• structured outputs
• human-in-the-loop enforcement

22. Development Stack
Current stack:

Backend
FastAPI (Python)

Database
PostgreSQL

Vector DB
Qdrant / PGVector

Inference
Local LLM runtime

Prompt Engine
Jinja2

23. Observability
System observability includes:

• agent execution logs
• system metrics
• error tracking
• AI invocation metrics

24. Future Extensibility
ALIS is designed to support:

• partner-developed modules
• custom institutional algorithms
• plugin agents

Extensions must operate within controlled runtime boundaries.

25. Summary
ALIS represents a new class of institutional software:

A governed AI operating system for universities.

It combines:

• deterministic governance
• modular orchestration
• AI assistance
• strict auditability

This architecture enables universities to automate operational complexity while preserving full institutional control.

